<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/baltimore-skyline.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/baltimore-skyline-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/baltimore-skyline.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/service-locations/">Locations</a></li><li aria-current="page">Baltimore</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Central Maryland · Baltimore City (independent city)</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser for Baltimore Rowhouse and Condo Claims</h1><p class="lede">Baltimore’s brick rowhouses, waterfront condos and century-old plaster interiors produce claims that generic estimating software often prices badly. We work as your insurance appraiser in Baltimore, MD when the carrier’s number and the real repair cost don’t meet.</p><div class="btns"><a class="btn brass" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/start-a-claim-review/">Request a free review</a></div><dl class="ph-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Baltimore City</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Central Maryland</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="localcard"><div><h2 class="lc-h">Local claim risks</h2><ul class="chips"><li>Flat-roof leaks</li><li>Party-wall fire and smoke</li><li>Harbor storm surge</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Derecho wind damage</li></ul></div><div><h2 class="lc-h">Storms on record</h2><ul class="dots"><li>Hurricane Isabel, 2003</li><li>February 2010 blizzards</li><li>June 2012 derecho</li><li>January 2016 blizzard</li></ul></div><div class="full"><h2 class="lc-h">Housing mix</h2><p>Mostly brick rowhouses built between the 1880s and 1920s, with detached homes in the northern neighborhoods and high-rise condos around the Inner Harbor and Harbor East.</p><p class="hoods"><b>Neighborhoods &amp; nearby:</b> Fells Point, Canton, Federal Hill, Hampden, Roland Park, Highlandtown, Mount Vernon, Locust Point</p></div></div><h2>Why Baltimore claims behave differently</h2>
<p>An insurance appraiser in Baltimore, MD spends much of the job on one building type: the brick rowhouse. Most of the city’s housing shares party walls with its neighbors, sits under a low-slope membrane or built-up roof, and still carries original plaster on lath behind the paint. Those details matter because carrier estimates are often written as if each house stood alone on a pitched shingle roof with drywall inside. When the scope starts from the wrong building, the dollar figure follows it.</p>
<h2>Rowhouse roofs, party walls and plaster</h2>
<p>A leak on a flat rowhouse roof rarely stays put. Water travels along the deck, into the parapet and down a shared wall, so the visible stain may be one room away from the failure. Disputes we see most often in the city include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Patch-only roof estimates where the membrane can’t be repaired without tearing off around flashings, parapets and the hatch</li>
<li>Drywall pricing for rooms finished in three-coat plaster, which costs more and takes a different trade</li>
<li>Fire and smoke claims that ignore odor and soot migration through a shared wall or cockloft</li>
<li>Code and permit items for older electrical and framing that surface once walls are opened</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/knowledge-base/baltimore-rowhouse-roof-claims/">Baltimore rowhouse roof claims</a> walks through what a complete flat-roof scope usually contains.</p>
<h2>Storm history written into the claim file</h2>
<p>Hurricane Isabel in 2003 pushed record surge up the Bay and flooded Fells Point and other low streets near the harbour. That event is a reminder that surge and rising water are generally treated as flood, which a standard homeowners policy excludes, while wind and wind-driven rain through a damaged roof may be covered. Sorting one from the other is often the real argument in waterfront claims.</p>
<p>Snow and wind events leave their own pattern. The back-to-back February 2010 blizzards and the January 2016 blizzard loaded flat roofs and drifted against parapets, and the June 2012 derecho toppled street trees onto rooftops and cars across the city. Ponding, split seams and tree-strike damage to cornices and rear additions are all common entries on Baltimore loss reports.</p>
<h2>Neighborhoods we cover in the city</h2>
<p>We inspect throughout Baltimore, from the harbour neighborhoods of Fells Point, Canton, Federal Hill and Locust Point to Highlandtown, Mount Vernon, Hampden and the larger detached homes of Roland Park. Condo associations around Harbor East and the Inner Harbor raise separate questions about the master policy versus the unit owner’s coverage, and we can look at both sides of that split.</p>
<h2>Practical steps after a Baltimore loss</h2>
<p>Photograph the roof surface and the parapets as soon as it is safe, not just the ceiling stain. If your neighbor’s house was affected too, note it, since shared-wall damage can involve two carriers. Keep samples of damaged plaster or trim if a contractor removes them. Before you sign anything, ask for the carrier’s full line-item estimate rather than a summary page.</p>
<p>Maryland Insurance Appraiser reviews that estimate against the actual building and tells you whether invoking appraisal makes sense. Appraisal decides the amount of loss; coverage questions and any bad-faith concerns belong with a Maryland attorney.</p><h2>Services for Baltimore property owners</h2><p>Every service is available in Baltimore and the rest of Baltimore City, including:</p><ul class="cols2"><li><a href="/property-claim-services/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Demand Representation</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/home-insurance-appraisal/">Home Insurance Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/roof-hail-damage-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/windstorm-tornado-appraisal/">Windstorm &amp; Tornado Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/hurricane-noreaster-appraisal/">Hurricane &amp; Nor’easter Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/property-claim-services/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Baltimore claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My rowhouse leak came through the neighbor’s side. Whose claim is it?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>It can involve both policies. Damage to your home is usually claimed on your policy, and your carrier may then seek recovery from the neighbor’s insurer if their roof or wall caused it. Document both sides and tell your adjuster about the shared wall early.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will my insurer pay to replace plaster rather than install drywall?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Policies generally pay to repair with materials of like kind and quality. In a plaster house that can support plaster pricing, or at least a fair blend, and this is a frequent point of disagreement that appraisal can resolve.</p></div></details><details><summary>Was flooding in Fells Point during Isabel covered by homeowners policies?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="a"><p>Storm surge is generally treated as flood, which standard homeowners policies exclude; flood coverage comes from a separate flood policy. Wind and rain damage from the same storm may still fall under the homeowners policy.</p></div></details></div><h2>Nearby service areas</h2><div class="rel"><a href="/service-locations/towson/"><small>Nearby</small><span>Towson</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/service-locations/ellicott-city/"><small>Nearby</small><span>Ellicott City</span><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a href="/service-locations/annapolis/"><small>Nearby</small><span>Annapo